    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A son of Huayna Cápac who spent his youth campaigning with his father in the north, Atahualpa governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> under his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> until their rivalry broke into civil war in 1529.</li><li>His generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcuchimac">Chalcuchímac</a> and Rumiñawi won the war in April 1532, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> and seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, just as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>'s small Spanish force was marching inland.</li><li>Seized in an ambush at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in November 1532, he offered a room of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for his life, but the Spaniards tried him for revolt, idolatry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>'s murder and garroted him in 1533 after his baptism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> word wallpa for 'chicken' was long thought to explain Atahualpa's name; since the bird was new to the Andes, scholars now think it more likely the bird was named after him, as Blas Valera said in the 16th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

